    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Births New Adventure Scooter Family, Three RW Models That Look More Like Motorcycles\nMINI Aceman, Electric Cooper, and Electric JCW Recalled in China for Detaching Rearview Mirrors\n95 photos\nPhoto: MINI / edited\nSome battery-electric MINIs are manufactured in China as part of a joint venture between Great Wall Motor and BMW. That arrangement explains why this MINI recall involves only domestically produced models, which may lose their rearview mirrors under normal driving scenarios due to insufficient bonding.\nSpotlight Automotive is the joint venture in question, and the expanded recall covers no fewer than 714 examples of the Aceman crossover, the full-electric Cooper , and zero-emission John Cooper Works models. The mounting bracket for the interior rearview mirror may not adhere properly, which means the mirror could obstruct the driver's view by loosening.\nA complete detachment is also possible, but unfortunately, the manufacturer did not explain in the filing published by the SAMR who is to blame for this bonding defect. S2026M0113V is what the State Administration for Market Regulation calls this campaign, which expands on a July 2026 recall.\nSpotlight Automotive's Zhangjiagang assembly complex is listed in the filing as the factory that produced the subject vehicles, with production dates ranging from April 7 through April 23, 2026, for the Cooper electrics. The JCW population was assembled between April 7 and April 10, 2026, while the subject Acemans were built from January 13 through April 23, 2026.\nRather than sticking the mirror back into place, mounting bracket and all, the remedy involves replacing the windshield. Spotlight Automotive tasked BMW Automotive Trading with replacing the assembly in full, and the filing published by the SAMR leaves a few questions unanswered.\nPhoto: MINI As noted earlier, the actual root cause remains unknown. The supplier of the adhesive and potential issues with the bonding process have not been detailed either, which is a bit uncanny if you ask me. Nevertheless, affected customers will have their vehicles fixed at no charge whatsoever.\nCustomers will be contacted by the folks at BMW China through registered mail, by telephone, and via SMS. In the meantime, customers may contact the automaker's local after-sales hotline for additional information. The Chinese State Administration for Market Regulation Recall Center is another option for them.\nInternally referred to as J01, the full-electric MINI Cooper looks extremely similar to its dinosaur juice-drinking brother. They are completely different models underneath, though, and you can thank BMW's joint venture with Great Wall Motor for this huge difference beneath the sheet metal.\nBack home in the United Kingdom, the most affordable Cooper E specification of the bunch will set you back 26,905 pounds sterling as opposed to 34,905 for the John Cooper Works. Both are front-wheel drive, and both offer relatively poor driving range. In the WLTP, their maximum estimates are 186 and 250 miles, respectively, meaning 300 and 402 kilometers in metric speak.\nThe Aceman crossover was developed on similar underpinnings, and you can't have it any other way than electric. For the Aceman E, prospective customers in the UK have to spend 29,060 pounds sterling at the very least. In return, they get a 38.5-kWh battery that promises no more than 192 miles (309 kilometers) on a full charge.\nVia // SAMR\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMini Cooper MINI JCW mini aceman Mini recall China\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT One-Owner 1963 Plymouth Fury Ragtop Sat 20 Years in a Barn, 140k-Mile Poly V8 Can Still Do a Burnout",
